five men accused of helping the 9 11  hijackers are due in a military court  today for the first time in a year and a  half the hearing in guantanamo bay cuba  comes near the 20th anniversary of the  terror attacks that killed nearly 3  thousand americans senior investigative  correspondent catherine herridge is in  cuba at guantanamo bay for us catherine  good morning to you what's behind all  these delays  tony the lawyers are still arguing over  the evidence and there is no date on the  calendar for the actual trial to begin  this pretrial hearing is the first time  the five defendants have gone to the  high security court since february last  year the latest judge on the case  retired and then the pandemic hit the  five defendants including the  self-described architect of the attack  khalid sheikh mohammed are accused of  war crimes among them conspiring to  commit acts of terrorism hijacking and  the intentional murder of civilians the  biggest legal bloc is the continued  secrecy surrounding the men's detention  before they got to guantanamo after 9 11  they were captured and held in cia  secret prisons where they were subjected  to interrogation tactics that critics  call torture including waterboarding and  sleep deprivation to this day much of  the evidence is off limits to the  defense these delays have gone on for so  long that i know some 911 family members  who have traveled to guantanamo to see  the defendants in court and have since  died never seeing justice in their  lifetime nate  catherine thank you so much
 

